version: "2.0"
name: gnomon-kernel-web
description: >-
  The localhost web endpoint: serves the GUI bundle (bundle.ts, gui-bundle.ts,
  gui-pin.ts), the launch capability and launch URL, the web protocol, and the
  resume-token auth that carries a GUI across a self-update (resume.ts).
context:
  - >-
    Split out of packages/gnomon/.purpose (2026-10-27); path: values stay
    relative to packages/gnomon, the package root.
components:
  web-endpoint:
    gui-bundle-binaries: >-
      #gui S1 (2026-10-20): the embedded GUI bundle carries binary files
      (woff2 fonts) and sub-directory paths (fonts/<name>). gui-bundle.ts
      stays opaque base64; materializeGuiBundle creates the sub-directories
      (0700 best-effort, never relied on) under <home>/gui/bundle/; the tree
      hash's SAFE_PATH still refuses `..`. rules.ts MIME map gains
      svg -> image/svg+xml for the bundle's own grain.svg (static only; /blob
      still never serves SVG); woff2 -> font/woff2 was already mapped. CSP
      unchanged (font-src 'self', img-src 'self' blob:).
    watermarks: >-
      T-2026-10-19-009 item 6 (§11.2 rule 12): the transport child reports
      `pressure` above writeHighWater (WS_WRITE_HIGH_WATER 1 MiB) and `drain`
      only at or below writeLowWater (WS_WRITE_LOW_WATER 256 KiB, a new
      ChildConfig field); 1008 past 8 MiB unchanged. The GUI WebSocket
      connection context carries writeLowWater for the log.read{follow}
      pump. The built-in GUI bundle was re-embedded (gui-bundle.ts) for the
      GUI client's DELIVERY_UNKNOWN change. Tests:
      test/web-backpressure.test.ts, test/web-child.test.ts.
    description: >-
      #web-endpoint (spec §2.1, §4.9 WebSocket paragraph, §11.2, §15.5
      `transport`, §2.4 linger; M1.4). The kernel half (src/kernel/web/) and
      the `gnomon-web` transport child (src/transport/). Listener
      (T-2026-10-18-020, §11.2 rule 1): the KERNEL listens on the 127.0.0.1
      literal (OS-chosen ephemeral port, pauseOnConnect) and hands each
      accepted socket, unread, to the child with subprocess.send('conn',
      socket) over the child's IPC channel (stdio fd 3, WEB_CONN_MESSAGE);
      accept-and-forward only, so the child stays the only parser (A24-1).
      The listener outlives a child restart (same port); stop(), the restart
      cap and a failed respawn close it (the port is then ECONNREFUSED, and
      squattable by a local process, §A.7); a connection accepted while no
      child serves is closed. Hand-off bounds (round 2, Aegis C1): listener
      maxConnections WEB_MAX_PENDING_HANDOFFS = 128 (Node counts a socket
      until it is sent and queues later hand-offs behind an unACKed one); an
      ACK watchdog (handoffAckMs, default 5 s, ACKs seen via the undocumented
      'internalMessage' NODE_HANDLE_ACK, FIFO) SIGKILLs a stalled child;
      any user IPC message and any internal one but NODE_HANDLE_ACK kills the
      child and closes an attached handle; an UNSOLICITED NODE_HANDLE_ACK
      (none in flight, or one more than sent) would make Node's own handler
      throw ERR_INTERNAL_ASSERTION in the daemon, so a prependListener
      (relies on the Node internal _handleQueue) gives Node an empty queue
      and kills the child (round 3); a failed send forgets its watchdog
      entry; a stop() during the exit-log write cancels the respawn and
      writes its stop row. Residual: Node's fd-3 JSON-line
      reader is unbounded (DoS-only, §A.7). A start that began before a
      stop() (stop generation counter) kills its child and never listens or
      writes a second socket.web.stop row; listenAddress() exposes the bound
      address. The CHILD listens on nothing and parses bytes only: node:http
      over the handed sockets (server.emit('connection')) with per-socket
      slowloris deadlines (a never-listening http.Server runs no
      headersTimeout/requestTimeout checker): headersTimeout 10 s from the
      hand-off and after each response, requestTimeout 30 s for the body;
      the ready record names no port (channel gnomon-web:v2), the RFC 6455 upgrade and zero-dep framing (ws-codec.ts: text only — binary
      1003; no fragmentation — FIN=0/continuation 1009; no extension ever
      negotiated — RSV bits 1002; masking required 1002; invalid UTF-8 1007;
      >4 MiB 1009 from the header; close mirrored; pings every 30 s, closes
      after 2 missed pongs), and static serving of the bundle bytes it hashed
      at start (held in memory). It forwards {rid, method, path, headers} /
      {rid, data} records over stdio (protocol.ts) and writes back only what
      the kernel says; its import closure is framing + channel + bundle hash
      (test/seams-fence.test.ts). The KERNEL decides every rule (rules.ts):
      Host = 127.0.0.1:<port> else 421 (host) before routing; Origin =
      http://127.0.0.1:<port> on every upgrade and non-static request else
      403 (origin); a present Sec-Fetch-Site same-origin|none else 403 (an
      absent one passes only an upgrade, and /diag and /blob, which then
      still need a live X-Gnomon-Token: Aegis finding 2); no cookies, no
      ACAO, the rule-6 CSP/COOP/CORP/nosniff/no-referrer on every response,
      no-store on non-static; traversal refused; /blob raster-only by content
      sniffing and verified content hash, GUI token in X-Gnomon-Token; /diag
      ≤ 64 KiB to <home>/logs/gui.log after redactDiagText (32-byte
      base64url tokens ⟨redacted⟩, contracts display sanitizer, Aegis C5); /blob and /diag are POST (a
      same-origin GET carries no Origin; the page sends them with
      referrerPolicy same-origin), GET/HEAD 405; /rpc and /diag match
      exactly, only /blob/ is a prefix; no HTTP RPC. Every child record is
      validated against the closed shape set (protocol.ts
      validateChildRecord) and the child is SIGKILLed on the first violation
      or on any throw in record handling (Aegis C1); a compromised child is a
      compromise of the GUI sessions it relays (spec §A.7). Child-written
      503/404 carry the rule-6 default headers. Per connection: the child
      starts at a 16 KiB inbound cap, raised by a kernel `limit` record on
      authentication; unsent bytes are reported as pressure/drain (the
      dispatcher's writableLength/onDrain are per connection) and over 8 MiB
      the child closes 1008; more than 32 queued requests close 1008. Read
      tokens do not authenticate on the web endpoint (Aegis C3). WebSocket: hello then
      auth within 5 s else 1008, frames before auth never dispatched as verbs;
      ≤ 64 live (non-closing) connections, at the cap the oldest live
      unauthenticated one is closed (1013), never an authenticated GUI one
      (503 instead); ≤ 5 auth
      attempts/min/connection. Its own dispatcher (authenticator signs
      ws://127.0.0.1:<port>/rpc) with a `web` auth hook (launch.ts): a
      single-use 60 s launch token minted in-process for an authenticated
      human device connection (DaemonHandle.requestWebLaunch — §5 names no
      verb for `gnomon open`), exchanged for a 32-byte GUI token held as a
      digest; a second presentation inside the TTL is theft (refused, the
      consuming session revoked, socket.auth.theft + socket.gui.session.ended
      {theft}); after the TTL a replay (socket.auth.replay only); both answer
      `replayed-transcript`. Reconnect logs socket.auth.reconnect.requested
      and is refused UNSUPPORTED web-auth-not-implemented {milestone M1.3,
      cause presence-unavailable} until the signed helper's presence. GUI
      sessions live as long as their socket (≤ 12 h; revoke-all; daemon
      exit). Bundle pin (bundle.ts): gnomon-bundle-tree:v1 hash; since
      M1.10 slice 1 the #gui bundle (packages/gnomon-gui, embedded as opaque
      base64 in the GENERATED gui-bundle.ts, read by gui-pin.ts outside the child closure) pinned by the GUI_BUNDLE_HASH
      literal until the root-signed `gnomon gui pin` (M1.3), materialized
      under <home>/gui/bundle/ (the placeholder bundle stays a fixture);
      a mismatch logs
      socket.web.bundle.mismatch and never opens. Supervision: restart cap
      3/10 min (socket.web.transport.exited, then socket.web.stop
      {transport-restart-cap}; a failed respawn ⇒ {transport-restart-failed};
      both clear the timers); 2-min linger after the last GUI session
      (socket.web.linger, socket.web.stop{no-sessions}). Production spawn
      only through the platform's `transport` sandbox profile
      (#oracle-sandbox; on macOS that profile has no network-* rule, so the
      child opens no network socket of its own — every bind, listen and
      outbound connect is EPERM, P14b-web, T-2026-10-18-020); the unsandboxed child exists only via the internal
      seam `web` (testing.ts createWebEndpointForTest). Event kinds emitted
      (§9.1 socket namespace): socket.web.listen, socket.web.bundle.mismatch,
      socket.web.linger, socket.web.stop, socket.web.transport.exited,
      socket.gui.token.issued, socket.gui.session.ended, socket.auth.theft,
      socket.auth.replay, socket.auth.reconnect.requested, socket.auth.fail
      {origin|host|sec-fetch-site|gui-token}. The spec declares no signals.
    path: src/kernel/web/
    type: module
    tags: [gnomon, kernel, web-endpoint, transport, paradigm-8, m1-4]
    tests:
      - test/web-units.test.ts
      - test/web-endpoint.test.ts
      - test/gui-endpoint.test.ts
      - test/web-child.test.ts
      - test/web-sandbox.test.ts
      - test/seams-fence.test.ts
      - test/refusal-reasons.test.ts
    oracles: [G-WEB-01, G-WEB-02, G-WEB-03, G-WEB-04, G-WEB-05, G-WEB-06, G-WEB-07, G-WEB-08]
  gui-resume:
    description: >-
      #gui-resume (TD-2026-10-19-063, founder ruling "ONE Touch ID") — the
      `{resumeToken}` web auth form (kernel/web/resume.ts). Minted only when
      an upgrade card is accepted from a live GUI session: 32 random bytes
      sent to THAT connection alone (gui.resume notification); the digest,
      bound to the session's principal, device id and root ns, is handed to
      the successor on its STDIN pipe together with its kernel key (Aegis M:
      never a file; a record planted in the home is never read, and none is
      read without --kernel-key-stdin). The successor serves on the old
      kernel's listening socket, inherited as fd 3 (--web-listen-fd), so the
      port is never unbound (Aegis H); on any other port the record is
      dropped. The GUI checks the successor signs a fresh nonce with the
      delivered key before it reloads. Refused if malformed, expired or with a
      TTL above RESUME_TOKEN_TTL_MS 60 s; accepted ONCE: replay, expiry, a device
      no longer that principal's human device, or another root ns are
      refused; RESUME_MAX_MISSES wrong tokens burn it. It authenticates a GUI
      connection only — never presence, never a card decision.
    tags: [kernel, web, auth]
